WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office Rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:19.410
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.550
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:24.890
Hello, everybody.

9
00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.030
Good morning.

10
00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:28.970
Today is very important.

11
00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:33.070
We have to do a very important legal procedure.

12
00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:34.430
Oh, Lord.

13
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:35.870
I was like, wait, what's happening?

14
00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:38.050
And then I realized you're talking about the episode.

15
00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:39.130
The deposition.

16
00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:40.990
I was like, Jenna, what do we have to do?

17
00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:42.010
Did I miss an email?

18
00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:42.970
What's happening?

19
00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:43.690
No.

20
00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:46.570
We have a very important deposition today, Angela.

21
00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.570
It's Season 4, Episode 12.

22
00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:49.870
Written by...

23
00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:51.230
Lester Lewis.

24
00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.630
Directed by Julian Farino.

25
00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:55.070
Hit me with a summary.

26
00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:56.150
Here it is.

27
00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.110
Michael and Jan go to corporate.

28
00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:04.810
And Michael is deposed as a witness in Jan's wrongful termination case against Dunder Mifflin.

29
00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:06.190
What was Jan thinking?

30
00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:09.650
Why would you ever put Michael on any kind of witness stand?

31
00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:10.990
I don't know.

32
00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:12.690
I'm so glad she did.

33
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:22.610
Well, while they're gone, Jim and Daryl play ping pong in the warehouse, which sparks a rivalry between Pam and Kelly.

34
00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:30.730
Fast fact number one this week, Angela, is Lester Lewis, the writer of this episode.

35
00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:37.050
Before writing on The Office, Lester had worked on The Larry Sanders Show and Caroline in the City.

36
00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:43.050
He was one of the original members of our writing team going back to Season 1.

37
00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.290
And this is a tough one for me, Ange.

38
00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:50.710
For both of us, I know Lester passed away in 2013.

39
00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.170
And I don't even know where to start.

40
00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:56.410
I loved him.

41
00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:57.650
I loved this man.

42
00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:02.230
We bonded over our love of autobiographical comic books.

43
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.150
We both loved those.

44
00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:08.230
And I just have such fond memories of being with him.

45
00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:09.390
And he was so funny.

46
00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:13.310
And he was so just kind and warm.

47
00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:14.810
He was so kind.

48
00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:17.130
And just a good, good person.

49
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:18.770
And I miss him.

50
00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:19.550
Yeah.

51
00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.470
I mean, Lester was someone that made time for everyone.

52
00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:23.470
You know those people?

53
00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:24.890
They make time for everybody.

54
00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.090
And he was just a wonderful part of our show.

55
00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:32.530
So I re-listened to the DVD commentary for this particular episode.

56
00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.250
Lester is in the commentary.

57
00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:39.690
And it was so wonderful to hear his voice and also made me sad.

58
00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:40.530
It was bittersweet.

59
00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:45.810
But if you listen to the DVD commentary for this episode, you will just hear what a thoughtful person he was.

60
00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:50.790
And I'll share a few of the things that he said on the commentary as we record today, Jenna.

61
00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:51.990
I love that.

62
00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:54.830
Well, lady, what is your fast fact number two?

63
00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:58.130
You know, ping pong features very heavily in this episode.

64
00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:02.210
So I felt we needed a little history of ping pong.

65
00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:03.050
Oh, my gosh.

66
00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:04.890
Did someone do a deep dive on ping pong?

67
00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:05.590
I did.

68
00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.610
I want you to know it has two other names.

69
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:09.350
Oh.

70
00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:12.010
Table tennis or whiff-waff.

71
00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:13.210
Whiff-waff?

72
00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:14.430
That sounds made up.

73
00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.470
Do you want to play some whiff-waff?

74
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.670
I'll play you some woof-le-whiff-waff.

75
00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:20.730
Oh, dear.

76
00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:22.210
I'll chunk it at you.

77
00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:24.510
What else you got about ping pong?

78
00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:27.250
Ping pong began in Victorian England.

79
00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:31.170
I know you'll like the sound of that, Angela, in the 1880s.

80
00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:36.770
When lawn tennis players adapted their game to play indoors during the winter.

81
00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:37.770
Oh.

82
00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:39.730
Where's my Netflix show about that?

83
00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:40.270
Right?

84
00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:41.490
Whiff-waff.

85
00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:42.630
Episode one.

86
00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:47.650
The Duke of Edinburgh comes to play whiff-waff.

87
00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:55.190
The game quickly caught on, and as early as 1901, tournaments were being conducted with

88
00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:57.110
over 300 participants.

89
00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:04.910
In 1902, a visiting Japanese university professor took the game back to Japan, where he introduced

90
00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.110
it to Japanese university students.

91
00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:11.470
The first world championships were held in London in 1927.

92
00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.250
They were won by a Hungarian, Dr. Jacobi.

93
00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:16.250
Congratulations.

94
00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:17.750
Dr. Jacobi.

95
00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:18.250
Mm-hmm.

96
00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:22.270
You go to see him for a checkup, but little do you know, he's a ping pong master.

97
00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:23.590
That's right.

98
00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:30.890
The Olympic Games started featuring ping pong for the first time in 1988 in Seoul.

99
00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:37.210
The television coverage of the men's singles final attracted a worldwide audience of two

100
00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:38.210
billion.

101
00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:39.270
Wow.

102
00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:40.230
Yeah.

103
00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:42.650
Here was something I found very interesting.

104
00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:47.730
There are many different ways to grip the paddle, and all of those grips have names.

105
00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:48.910
Oh, gosh.

106
00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:50.330
Here's how you can hold it.

107
00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:55.890
You can give it the old pen hold, the shake hand, or the C-Miller.

108
00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:02.250
The C-Miller is actually named after an American table tennis champ, Danny C-Miller, because that's

109
00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:03.330
how he held his paddle.

110
00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:05.210
He puts his little finger out.

111
00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:06.510
That's what she said.

112
00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:12.050
There are also a bunch of different strokes.

113
00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:12.970
Oh, gosh.

114
00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:14.270
That's what she said.

115
00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:15.130
Oh, Lord.

116
00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:20.370
There are offensive strokes, the hit, the loop, the counter hit, the flip, the smash.

117
00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:26.050
Then there's defensive strokes called the push, the chop, the block, and the lob.

118
00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:31.750
And the biggest part of the game, the biggest thing that these guys work on is their spin.

119
00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:37.830
And you can do a top spin, a back spin, a side spin, and a cork spin.

120
00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.770
Do you remember how good Forrest Gump was at ping pong?

121
00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:41.590
Yeah.

122
00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:43.150
He was so good.

123
00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:45.190
I wonder if he was using the C-Miller.

124
00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:46.770
Maybe the pen hold.

125
00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:47.630
I don't know.

126
00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:52.330
I think you do know that my husband turned our beautiful dining table into a ping pong table.

127
00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:53.950
Lady, I know this.

128
00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:58.070
I watched this happen, and I knew you were going to regret it.

129
00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:02.430
You guys were real excited, and I thought, hmm, that's going to come back.

130
00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:04.190
They haven't regretted it, Jenna.

131
00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:06.490
There's only one person that regrets it.

132
00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.750
Can we explain what Angela did, guys?

133
00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:10.330
Here's what her family did.

134
00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:16.470
They got one of these ping pong table tops that fits over her kitchen table.

135
00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:19.210
It's meant to go over a pool table, right?

136
00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:19.770
Oh.

137
00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:21.870
It's meant to set on top of a pool table.

138
00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:26.410
Josh got it because, you know, we've all been quarantining and at home, and so we're

139
00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:27.650
running out of things to do.

140
00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.230
Kids are bouncing off the walls.

141
00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:32.970
So we bought this thing because it's way less expensive than buying an actual ping pong

142
00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.470
table, and you just put it on top of your dining table.

143
00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:38.510
Of course, it doesn't fit our dining table, so it hangs off.

144
00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:42.710
Well, also, now, guys, she has nowhere to eat, okay?

145
00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.610
Angela, this is now your dining table.

146
00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:48.830
It's just, it's so, I don't know.

147
00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:50.790
Josh and the kids love it.

148
00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:57.890
What I didn't factor in, I knew I'd be eating, like, awkwardly for the rest of our time, but

149
00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:01.510
what I didn't factor in was the bouncing ball sound.

150
00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:02.130
Mm-hmm.

151
00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:04.170
I'm talking at, like, 8 a.m.

152
00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:06.430
because they want to play a game before school starts.

153
00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:06.910
Mm-hmm.

154
00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:07.270
You know?

155
00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:14.790
So it's like, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank,

156
00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:18.510
bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank.

157
00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:20.290
It is non-stop.

158
00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:20.850
Yeah.

159
00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:22.030
You come down the stairs.

160
00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:23.950
Bank, bank, bank, bank, bank, bank.

161
00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:25.410
It doesn't matter what time.

162
00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:28.570
You've got that, and then all your appliances are beeping.

163
00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:30.530
Oh, don't get me started.

164
00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:31.870
It's so much noise.

165
00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:35.790
Anyway, here's an interesting tidbit from the commentary.

166
00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:39.970
Jenna, I know how much you love the wall in the writer's room with all the note cards,

167
00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:40.370
right?

168
00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:41.010
Yeah.

169
00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:44.130
All the different possible story ideas.

170
00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:47.050
It's one of my favorite things to go look at at my lunch.

171
00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:51.170
Well, Lester shared, there was one for this episode that didn't get used.

172
00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:55.570
Simply written, it was, Dwight gets stuck in the wall.

173
00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:56.670
What?

174
00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:05.030
If we didn't have this ping pong competition, the other thing that might have happened is

175
00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:06.950
that Dwight was going to get stuck in a wall.

176
00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:07.530
Yeah.

177
00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:09.690
That was going to be Dwight's storyline.

178
00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:13.090
Instead of helping Jim or the ping pong, I don't know how it would have played out.

179
00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:16.390
Ultimately, they couldn't make it work in this episode.

180
00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:17.010
So.

181
00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:18.030
Oh, man.

182
00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.830
I have to say, I love this ping pong story.

183
00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:25.750
I also would have loved to have seen whatever that was going to be.

184
00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:27.630
How would he have gotten in the wall?

185
00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:29.330
How would he have got stuck in there?

186
00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:31.590
I was laughing so hard when they were talking about it.

187
00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:33.230
That is some good stuff.

188
00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:34.150
All right.

189
00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:38.750
Fast fact number three is all about the writer's strike.

190
00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:39.730
Oh.

191
00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:41.030
Mm-hmm.

192
00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:47.630
This was the last episode of The Office that we shot before the Writers Guild of America

193
00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:48.750
went on strike.

194
00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:50.550
And we had to shut down production.

195
00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:57.330
Now, this was really significant, guys, because NBC ordered 30 episodes for season four of

196
00:08:57.330 --> 00:08:57.790
The Office.

197
00:08:58.170 --> 00:08:58.470
30.

198
00:08:58.730 --> 00:08:59.190
Wow.

199
00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.470
This was a huge episode order.

200
00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:05.470
You know, typical seasons are like 22 to 24 episodes long.

201
00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:09.470
But then, in November 2007, the Writers Guild went on strike.

202
00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:15.570
So, if I remember correctly, Angela, we were allowed to shoot any outstanding scripts, but

203
00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:20.210
there could be no new writing done, and we could not have a writer on set with us.

204
00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:20.870
Exactly.

205
00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:22.970
No writers were showing up to work, period.

206
00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:27.010
Well, we really relied on our writers to be on set with us while we were shooting.

207
00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:32.230
That was a big part of our process on the show, is they would pitch alternates, and

208
00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:35.330
they were so important to the show being great.

209
00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:38.110
And not only that, three of them were also actors.

210
00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:43.430
And Steve Carell was a member of the Writers Guild Union, and he would not cross the picket

211
00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:44.150
line for work.

212
00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:45.150
That's right.

213
00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:48.890
And I know that meant a lot to our writers that he stood with them.

214
00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:49.590
Yes.

215
00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:55.470
So, we shut down, and any unused scripts were just tabled, and the strike lasted over three

216
00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:55.910
months.

217
00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:56.810
Yes.

218
00:09:57.030 --> 00:10:01.950
And for those of you that are on the old Preggers belly tracker, the Kinsey

219
00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:08.330
Preggers belly, this was really weird, because I left work, not really showing, and just

220
00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:09.270
wait for dinner party.

221
00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:17.850
Well, Angela, you also went out with some of the other actors, and you picketed, I remember.

222
00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:18.750
I did.

223
00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:23.150
I went over, and I picketed with our writers, and I remember Ed was there the day that I

224
00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:26.090
went, and it was a very bonding experience.

225
00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:29.090
The Writers Guild wasn't asking for the moon.

226
00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:33.990
They were just asking for really just basic, decent, fair treatment from studios, and I

227
00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:37.930
was really happy to help get their voices heard in any way that I could.

228
00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:42.430
Well, we always appreciated our writers, but I'll tell you, Angela, you and I are writing

229
00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:43.350
a book right now.

230
00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:44.090
Ooh.

231
00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:51.950
And I will say, there is nothing harder than staring at a blank piece of paper and putting

232
00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:52.710
something on it.

233
00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:53.170
Yeah.

234
00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:54.030
Yeah, I know.

235
00:10:54.190 --> 00:11:00.030
I think that writers have the hardest job in our industry, because all the rest of us,

236
00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:01.870
we get to start with something.

237
00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:07.830
We get to build on something that already exists, but the writers have to manifest it from nothing.

238
00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:09.030
Well, yeah, I know.

239
00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:11.650
After the office, I went into development, as you know, Jenna.

240
00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:17.670
I wrote, and I sold pilots, and it was so hard, because not only do you write this thing that

241
00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:22.690
you love, it becomes your baby, then you have to hand it over to, like, studio heads

242
00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:26.410
and network, and they have all these notes, and they just carve it up, and you're like,

243
00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:27.110
my baby!

244
00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:28.070
Yeah.

245
00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:30.010
Guys, we love our writers.

246
00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:30.710
Yep.

247
00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:35.670
And a lot of people always ask, why was season four of The Office so much shorter than the

248
00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:35.990
others?

249
00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:37.170
And that is why.

250
00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:38.470
Because of the writer's strike.

251
00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:38.930
Yep.

252
00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:40.730
We made fewer episodes that year.

253
00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:44.250
Well, Jenna, we were very lucky, because our show survived the writer's strike.

